WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.167 --> 00:00:02.702
- Honestly, I think
many, many people

00:00:02.702 --> 00:00:04.070
would be far better off not
going to university at all,

00:00:04.070 --> 00:00:05.605 align:start size:97% position:3%
and I think it's an important
point for me to make

00:00:05.605 --> 00:00:07.173
because here I am part of a
team founding a new college.

00:00:07.173 --> 00:00:09.109
It might make it seem like I
think college is the answer.

00:00:09.109 --> 00:00:12.512
I think by and large
most, if not most,

00:00:12.512 --> 00:00:15.882 align:start size:84% position:16%
very, very many
people can and should

00:00:15.882 --> 00:00:18.685 align:start position:0%
and will lead higher and
better and more beautiful lives

00:00:18.685 --> 00:00:19.819
by not going to college.

00:00:19.819 --> 00:00:21.388 align:start size:84% position:16%
And we need to
remember that, right?

00:00:21.388 --> 00:00:22.822
We don't want to become a
place that somehow thinks

00:00:22.822 --> 00:00:25.158
we're all just minds, or
that everyone has a mind

00:00:25.158 --> 00:00:26.393 align:start size:84% position:16%
that is good at
thinking about this.

00:00:26.393 --> 00:00:28.828
We have people who,
in the true diversity

00:00:28.828 --> 00:00:30.630
of the human race, many
people who are doing

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:32.565
many different kinds of
things, and most of them,

00:00:32.565 --> 00:00:34.134 align:start size:94% position:6%
by the way, don't
involve going to college.

00:00:34.134 --> 00:00:36.703
(gentle music)

00:00:41.174 --> 00:00:43.943
- I'm Dave Rubin, and we
are at the Locals studio

00:00:43.943 --> 00:00:47.647 align:start size:97% position:3%
here in Miami, and I am
joined by Stephen Blackwood,

00:00:47.647 --> 00:00:50.683 align:start size:97% position:3%
the Co-Founder and
President of Ralston College

00:00:50.683 --> 00:00:52.385
in Savannah, Georgia.

00:00:52.385 --> 00:00:54.854 align:start size:94% position:6%
Stephen, we're almost
dressed exactly the same.

00:00:54.854 --> 00:00:55.889
It's nice to see you.

00:00:55.889 --> 00:00:57.524
- Good to be here, thank you.

00:00:57.524 --> 00:01:00.160 align:start size:84% position:16%
- So we've only done
one show together

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:03.296
in my many, many years
of interviewing people.

00:01:03.296 --> 00:01:05.165 align:start size:84% position:16%
So I want to talk
about Ralston College,

00:01:05.165 --> 00:01:09.302 align:start size:97% position:3%
a place of actual learning,
from what I understand.

00:01:09.302 --> 00:01:11.538
And Jordan Peterson's
intimately involved

00:01:11.538 --> 00:01:13.173 align:start size:97% position:3%
in what you guys are doing,
so I know this will be

00:01:13.173 --> 00:01:14.507
interesting to my audience.

00:01:14.507 --> 00:01:18.378
So first off, give me a
one minute bio on you,

00:01:18.378 --> 00:01:19.879 align:start size:84% position:16%
how you ended up
starting this place,

00:01:19.879 --> 00:01:22.482
and then we'll talk about
what's going on over there.

00:01:22.482 --> 00:01:26.219 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Gosh, well, in some
sense I'm just a farm boy

00:01:26.219 --> 00:01:30.557 align:start size:88% position:13%
who discovered big
ideas and the difference

00:01:30.557 --> 00:01:33.860
that education made in my life

00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:36.196 align:start size:94% position:6%
and that I believe can
make in the lives of many,

00:01:36.196 --> 00:01:39.199
the role it plays in a culture.

00:01:39.199 --> 00:01:41.134 align:start size:94% position:6%
So I had my own
trajectory educationally.

00:01:41.134 --> 00:01:45.105 align:start size:84% position:16%
I studied philosophy
and classics,

00:01:45.105 --> 00:01:48.675
worked in the inner city
for a while, lived abroad,

00:01:48.675 --> 00:01:52.712
did a PhD in Atlanta
at Emory University,

00:01:52.712 --> 00:01:57.617
and basically, sort of
with the other founders

00:01:57.617 --> 00:01:59.018
of the college, sort of
surveyed the landscape

00:01:59.018 --> 00:02:01.855
of higher education, in
which there are many,

00:02:01.855 --> 00:02:03.556 align:start size:84% position:16%
let's say, chronic
and systemic problems.

00:02:03.556 --> 00:02:06.092
- That would be one
way of putting it.

00:02:06.092 --> 00:02:07.694 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Well, you know,
let's maybe understate

00:02:07.694 --> 00:02:08.828
rather than overstate, but yeah,

00:02:08.828 --> 00:02:10.497 align:start size:84% position:16%
let's say the
problems are serious.

00:02:10.497 --> 00:02:13.867 align:start size:84% position:16%
But rather than focus
on the problems,

00:02:13.867 --> 00:02:16.069
we thought, well, maybe we
can do something positive.

00:02:16.069 --> 00:02:17.537
What could we do?

00:02:17.537 --> 00:02:20.740 align:start position:0%
It's always easy to kind of
bitch and complain about things,

00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:22.408 align:start size:88% position:13%
and frankly I think
there's too much of that

00:02:22.408 --> 00:02:23.610
in our world generally.

00:02:23.610 --> 00:02:25.945 align:start size:88% position:13%
And so we thought, well,
let's see if we can

00:02:25.945 --> 00:02:28.381
do something better, more
beautiful, more wonderful,

00:02:28.381 --> 00:02:30.016 align:start size:84% position:16%
more transformative
for students.

00:02:30.016 --> 00:02:33.653 align:start size:88% position:13%
And that was
essentially the impetus.

00:02:33.653 --> 00:02:37.390
- So how you got on my radar
was Jordan Peterson said to me

00:02:37.390 --> 00:02:38.958 align:start size:84% position:16%
do you know this
Stephen Blackwood guy?

00:02:38.958 --> 00:02:41.494
Do you know what's
going on at Ralston?

00:02:41.494 --> 00:02:44.964 align:start size:94% position:6%
And you are here actually
in Miami right now

00:02:44.964 --> 00:02:48.234 align:start size:97% position:3%
because you're doing the
Biblical series with Jordan.

00:02:48.234 --> 00:02:50.770
You're about to finish
it up this afternoon.

00:02:50.770 --> 00:02:52.372 align:start size:97% position:3%
Before we do all the
Ralston stuff and education

00:02:52.372 --> 00:02:54.574 align:start size:97% position:3%
and all that, can you just
talk about the Biblical series

00:02:54.574 --> 00:02:56.009
and what that's been like being

00:02:56.009 --> 00:02:59.112 align:start size:97% position:3%
with these cast of characters,
Shapiro's been there,

00:02:59.112 --> 00:03:02.649
and Prager, and a whole
bunch of great thinkers.

00:03:02.649 --> 00:03:04.417
- Well, I mean, I think this is

00:03:04.417 --> 00:03:08.621
a really interesting
phenomenon, frankly.

00:03:08.621 --> 00:03:12.258 align:start size:94% position:6%
As everyone knows, Dr.
Peterson, one of his first

00:03:12.258 --> 00:03:14.928
really big works was
a series of lectures

00:03:14.928 --> 00:03:16.829
he gave on the book of Genesis.

00:03:16.829 --> 00:03:19.365
And I think the story
is he just thought

00:03:19.365 --> 00:03:23.403 align:start position:0%
that maybe it was possible that
this kind of perennial text

00:03:23.403 --> 00:03:27.340 align:start size:97% position:3%
that people had been reading
for more than 2000 years,

00:03:27.340 --> 00:03:30.610
was there a way it might
speak to a wide audience?

00:03:30.610 --> 00:03:33.746
He just gave it his best shot
at trying to make sense of it.

00:03:33.746 --> 00:03:37.217 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I think the story was
that he just booked a theater

00:03:37.217 --> 00:03:40.153 align:start size:97% position:3%
for 16 nights or
something, and it sold out.

00:03:40.153 --> 00:03:41.854
And I think that's actually
quite an important moment

00:03:41.854 --> 00:03:46.859 align:start size:84% position:16%
in Dr. Peterson's
own thinking through

00:03:48.294 --> 00:03:51.931
what his own vocation is and
what the work of our time is.

00:03:51.931 --> 00:03:54.467
I can't speak for him of course,

00:03:54.467 --> 00:03:57.904
but I think it's a pretty
simple formula in a way.

00:03:57.904 --> 00:03:59.706
There are all kinds of
wonderful treasures,

00:03:59.706 --> 00:04:02.842 align:start position:0%
things figured out in the past,
great books, works of art,

00:04:02.842 --> 00:04:06.446
ways of understanding,
cultural, let's say,

00:04:08.181 --> 00:04:11.751 align:start size:84% position:16%
cultural wisdom that
it's just there.

00:04:11.751 --> 00:04:13.019
It's just there.

00:04:13.019 --> 00:04:14.387
I mean, we're not
living in a place,

00:04:14.387 --> 00:04:17.323
the situation is not
that we're in a desert

00:04:17.323 --> 00:04:18.791
or we're at the
beginning of time

00:04:18.791 --> 00:04:20.093 align:start size:88% position:13%
and nothing's ever
been done and we've got

00:04:20.093 --> 00:04:21.928
to figure out how to do it all.

00:04:21.928 --> 00:04:26.933
The situation is as
if everyone's hungry

00:04:28.001 --> 00:04:30.003 align:start size:88% position:13%
and you're told
there's nothing to eat,

00:04:30.003 --> 00:04:33.439
there's no food,
we're in a famine.

00:04:33.439 --> 00:04:37.477
But actually go over here, and
you open this door over here.

00:04:37.477 --> 00:04:40.513
There's the biggest,
most beautiful feast

00:04:40.513 --> 00:04:42.815
ever known to man all here,
anything you could imagine,

00:04:42.815 --> 00:04:47.820
the most beautifully prepared
and gorgeously grown produce

00:04:48.621 --> 00:04:50.023
and so on and so forth.

00:04:50.023 --> 00:04:52.292 align:start position:0%
I don't believe in the metaphor,
but the point I'm making

00:04:52.292 --> 00:04:54.961 align:start size:84% position:16%
is that in fact
we've got this tray.

00:04:54.961 --> 00:04:58.431
You could change this to
think we have no beauty,

00:04:58.431 --> 00:05:00.500
and say, well actually, look
at all these amazing things.

00:05:00.500 --> 00:05:01.768
So the point is a simple one.

00:05:01.768 --> 00:05:03.836
It is that I think
the Exodus seminar

00:05:03.836 --> 00:05:06.306
has been born of a
very simple insight,

00:05:06.306 --> 00:05:09.309
and that is that these
works that human beings

00:05:09.309 --> 00:05:11.978 align:start size:97% position:3%
have found
transformatively insightful

00:05:11.978 --> 00:05:16.649
to make sense of their own
lives for 2000, 3000 years,

00:05:16.649 --> 00:05:18.284
that they can still speak today.

00:05:18.284 --> 00:05:23.222
And rather than simply tell
people what they're about,

00:05:23.222 --> 00:05:26.826 align:start size:97% position:3%
what happens if we just have
a conversation about it?

00:05:26.826 --> 00:05:28.594
Does that come alive in a way

00:05:28.594 --> 00:05:30.229
that it then comes alive for us?

00:05:30.229 --> 00:05:33.866
And and my experience
in this last week or so

00:05:33.866 --> 00:05:37.904
that we've been here is
that this conversation,

00:05:37.904 --> 00:05:41.207
which I would say has very
low to no levels of ego,

00:05:41.207 --> 00:05:43.476
which is sincerely just
trying to figure out

00:05:43.476 --> 00:05:47.213 align:start size:97% position:3%
this amazing text and see what
we can understand about it

00:05:47.213 --> 00:05:50.817
has been a really beautiful
and memorable experience.

00:05:50.817 --> 00:05:53.386 align:start size:97% position:3%
And it's a very interesting
thing to note, Dave,

00:05:53.386 --> 00:05:56.489
that there appears to be
a wide audience of people

00:05:56.489 --> 00:05:59.726
who are hungry for that
kind of non-instrumental

00:05:59.726 --> 00:06:04.664
not reductively political,
richly expansive deep content

00:06:06.065 --> 00:06:06.866 align:start size:94% position:6%
that says something to
them about what the nature

00:06:06.866 --> 00:06:08.034
of human life is.

00:06:08.034 --> 00:06:09.702 align:start size:84% position:16%
- To bring that
metaphor to everything

00:06:09.702 --> 00:06:11.237
that's going on in
the world today,

00:06:11.237 --> 00:06:13.206
I mean, it sort of feels to
me like we've been wandering

00:06:13.206 --> 00:06:16.409
in the desert with a lot
of lies, a lot of untruths,

00:06:16.409 --> 00:06:19.312
half truths, a lot of confusion.

00:06:19.312 --> 00:06:21.114
But there does seem
to be a promised land

00:06:21.114 --> 00:06:22.248
on the other side of that,

00:06:22.248 --> 00:06:24.584 align:start size:88% position:13%
where people are
seriously talking about

00:06:24.584 --> 00:06:28.221 align:start size:94% position:6%
how to rebuild things,
how to get us to that land

00:06:28.221 --> 00:06:30.289
where there is goodness
and all of those things.

00:06:30.289 --> 00:06:32.058 align:start size:94% position:6%
What's your role
specifically at the table,

00:06:32.058 --> 00:06:34.026
because everyone sort
of has a little bit

00:06:34.026 --> 00:06:35.428
of their own expertise, right?

00:06:35.428 --> 00:06:37.230
- Yeah, I think that's right.

00:06:37.230 --> 00:06:40.066
My background really is in
philosophy and theology.

00:06:40.066 --> 00:06:42.635
So I'm a kind of a
history of ideas,

00:06:42.635 --> 00:06:45.671
what are the fundamental
questions kind of person.

00:06:45.671 --> 00:06:48.040
But I would say in this seminar,

00:06:48.040 --> 00:06:50.209 align:start size:84% position:16%
I found myself turning
again and again

00:06:50.209 --> 00:06:53.279 align:start size:94% position:6%
to ask the kind of
almost personal question,

00:06:53.279 --> 00:06:55.515
what does this say
to me right now?

00:06:55.515 --> 00:07:00.486
And I suppose I think that's,
in a way, the big question.

00:07:01.654 --> 00:07:03.656
I'm also aware that on
the Daily Wire Plus,

00:07:03.656 --> 00:07:06.259
there's gonna be a lot of
people listening to this

00:07:06.259 --> 00:07:09.495 align:start size:97% position:3%
not simply for, let's say,
academic or scholarly reasons.

00:07:09.495 --> 00:07:10.963 align:start size:88% position:13%
They're trying to
figure out their lives,

00:07:10.963 --> 00:07:12.899 align:start size:97% position:3%
just as I'm trying to figure
out mine and we all are.

00:07:12.899 --> 00:07:16.169
So I think what I've
tried to do, at least,

00:07:16.169 --> 00:07:18.638
is trying to approach
things from that angle.

00:07:18.638 --> 00:07:20.907 align:start size:88% position:13%
- All right, so let's
bring that conversation,

00:07:20.907 --> 00:07:23.009 align:start size:97% position:3%
this wandering in the desert
we all seem to be doing,

00:07:23.009 --> 00:07:26.345 align:start size:88% position:13%
to why you would start,
co-found a school.

00:07:26.345 --> 00:07:29.048
I mean, a lot of it is
because the information

00:07:29.048 --> 00:07:30.716
is all nonsensical, right?

00:07:30.716 --> 00:07:33.686
Everyone in the world
seems to be wandering

00:07:33.686 --> 00:07:35.087
in that desert academically,

00:07:35.087 --> 00:07:37.290 align:start size:84% position:16%
and there aren't
many promised lands.

00:07:37.290 --> 00:07:39.392 align:start size:88% position:13%
Did I set this up
nicely for you, or what?

00:07:39.392 --> 00:07:41.427
- I think it's very important.

00:07:41.427 --> 00:07:43.629
I think actually
Exodus does give us

00:07:43.629 --> 00:07:45.798 align:start size:94% position:6%
really fundamental
language of the landscape

00:07:45.798 --> 00:07:47.300
of our own experience, right?

00:07:47.300 --> 00:07:51.003
We all, in some sense, are
living in states of longing,

00:07:51.003 --> 00:07:53.206
or you might say the desert,

00:07:53.206 --> 00:07:55.441
the place where we're
trying to find our way,

00:07:55.441 --> 00:07:57.076 align:start size:84% position:16%
we're confused, we're
battering around,

00:07:57.076 --> 00:07:58.811
we're lonely, we're
alienated, whatever,

00:07:58.811 --> 00:08:01.514 align:start size:84% position:16%
we've got our personal
issues and so on.

00:08:01.514 --> 00:08:02.748
And then the question is, well,

00:08:02.748 --> 00:08:05.051 align:start size:81% position:19%
how do you make
sense out of that?

00:08:05.051 --> 00:08:07.653 align:start size:97% position:3%
And that is I think, in a
way, what the book of Exodus

00:08:07.653 --> 00:08:10.223
is itself, at least
taken historically

00:08:10.223 --> 00:08:13.559
by people of various faiths,
to be a kind of revelation

00:08:13.559 --> 00:08:18.331 align:start size:84% position:16%
about how one can
move from that state.

00:08:18.331 --> 00:08:20.533 align:start size:88% position:13%
And it doesn't come
down just to ourselves,

00:08:20.533 --> 00:08:21.901
by the way, either.

00:08:21.901 --> 00:08:23.402 align:start size:94% position:6%
But relative to education
and the founding

00:08:23.402 --> 00:08:26.138
of Ralston College, what I'd
say is a couple of things.

00:08:26.138 --> 00:08:29.041 align:start size:88% position:13%
First is at a big
systemic cultural level,

00:08:29.041 --> 00:08:30.409 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think we've got to be
really honest with ourselves

00:08:30.409 --> 00:08:32.945
and say right now,
the West generally,

00:08:32.945 --> 00:08:35.081 align:start size:94% position:6%
the United States and
any other country I know,

00:08:35.081 --> 00:08:37.083
so by and large the countries
of North America and Europe

00:08:37.083 --> 00:08:40.753
principally, we do not now have

00:08:40.753 --> 00:08:44.090 align:start size:97% position:3%
the mechanisms of transmission
for our own culture.

00:08:45.391 --> 00:08:48.027 align:start size:97% position:3%
So let's take the constitution
of the United States

00:08:48.027 --> 00:08:50.897
or the forms of building
things that are beautiful

00:08:50.897 --> 00:08:54.667
or the forms of ritual
that have defined life.

00:08:54.667 --> 00:08:57.970 align:start size:97% position:3%
You know, in profound ways,
these have been eroded

00:08:57.970 --> 00:09:00.439 align:start size:88% position:13%
or degraded or
abandoned or forgotten,

00:09:00.439 --> 00:09:04.143
and one cannot overstate
what a problem that is.

00:09:04.143 --> 00:09:08.347 align:start size:94% position:6%
You cannot have a culture
without mechanisms

00:09:08.347 --> 00:09:10.149
of transmission of that culture.

00:09:10.149 --> 00:09:14.220 align:start size:97% position:3%
It's like imagine you're
French or German or whatever.

00:09:14.220 --> 00:09:15.855 align:start size:84% position:16%
If you stop speaking
that language,

00:09:15.855 --> 00:09:17.690
it's gone within a generation.

00:09:17.690 --> 00:09:22.395 align:start size:81% position:19%
And so all the
culture really is,

00:09:22.395 --> 00:09:26.365 align:start position:0%
the late philosopher, Sir Roger
Scruton put it beautifully.

00:09:26.365 --> 00:09:29.669
He said in a way a culture is
just the things we have loved.

00:09:29.669 --> 00:09:34.574
But you know, the only way
something that we have loved

00:09:34.574 --> 00:09:37.476
gets transmitted is by teaching
someone else to love it too.

00:09:37.476 --> 00:09:42.481 align:start position:0%
And right now, instead of a
kind of, not slavish obedience,

00:09:43.783 --> 00:09:47.553
but humble openness and
respect to the past,

00:09:47.553 --> 00:09:50.623
has been replaced in many
respects with ignorance

00:09:50.623 --> 00:09:53.926
or antagonism, or this was
just a history of oppression.

00:09:53.926 --> 00:09:58.764
I think, well, this is a
profoundly insidious way

00:09:59.966 --> 00:10:02.401
of regarding all of the
things that human beings

00:10:02.401 --> 00:10:05.738
up until now could teach
us if we would just listen.

00:10:05.738 --> 00:10:08.674 align:start size:97% position:3%
So I make that general point
about the systemic thing

00:10:08.674 --> 00:10:11.544
because I want to be very
clear that this is not

00:10:11.544 --> 00:10:14.513
kind of window dressing, or
we could kind of have this

00:10:14.513 --> 00:10:15.748
or have it that way.

00:10:15.748 --> 00:10:18.918 align:start size:88% position:13%
I'm talking about the
most fundamental reality

00:10:18.918 --> 00:10:20.820
of the world in which we live,

00:10:20.820 --> 00:10:23.022
and the very conditions
of human flourishing

00:10:23.022 --> 00:10:25.858 align:start size:94% position:6%
as they've been understood
in the United States

00:10:25.858 --> 00:10:27.126
and in the West.

00:10:27.126 --> 00:10:30.563
I would say those have
been or are forgotten

00:10:30.563 --> 00:10:31.897
in fundamental ways.

00:10:31.897 --> 00:10:34.033
The next thing I would
say is you can have this

00:10:34.033 --> 00:10:36.669
sort of analysis that
there are problems

00:10:36.669 --> 00:10:38.537
in this field or this
field or that domain

00:10:38.537 --> 00:10:40.373
or in this industry or whatever,

00:10:40.373 --> 00:10:45.344 align:start size:84% position:16%
but even a good grasp
on the problems,

00:10:45.344 --> 00:10:47.546 align:start size:88% position:13%
a good kind of
critical understanding,

00:10:47.546 --> 00:10:49.081
that only takes you so far

00:10:49.081 --> 00:10:52.585
because you can't build
out of a negative vision.

00:10:52.585 --> 00:10:54.120
You can't build
out of criticism.

00:10:54.120 --> 00:10:56.489
So I often give the example,
I make no bones about the fact

00:10:56.489 --> 00:10:58.090 align:start size:88% position:13%
that I don't like
brutalist architecture.

00:10:58.090 --> 00:11:00.526 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think it's ugly, I think
it's soul destroying,

00:11:00.526 --> 00:11:01.861
I think it's alienating.

00:11:01.861 --> 00:11:04.063
It has no way in to
understand yourself.

00:11:04.063 --> 00:11:06.565
It's kind of demeaning
rather than elevating.

00:11:06.565 --> 00:11:09.835
But you know, not liking
brutalist architecture

00:11:09.835 --> 00:11:11.904 align:start size:84% position:16%
allows you to build
exactly zero buildings

00:11:11.904 --> 00:11:13.105
that are different, right?

00:11:13.105 --> 00:11:14.340 align:start size:81% position:19%
You've got to say,
well you know,

00:11:14.340 --> 00:11:16.375 align:start size:84% position:16%
I'm for a certain
vision of proportion

00:11:16.375 --> 00:11:17.877
and symmetry and so on.

00:11:17.877 --> 00:11:19.645
So what we're trying to
do is something positive,

00:11:19.645 --> 00:11:21.814
and that positive,
I want to just say

00:11:21.814 --> 00:11:23.683 align:start size:81% position:19%
to the third point
I want to make

00:11:23.683 --> 00:11:25.117
is you can talk about systems,

00:11:25.117 --> 00:11:27.086 align:start size:97% position:3%
you can talk about positive
vision and everything,

00:11:27.086 --> 00:11:28.587
but do you know what?

00:11:28.587 --> 00:11:29.822
At the end of the day, this
comes down to individuals.

00:11:29.822 --> 00:11:33.025 align:start size:81% position:19%
And the way you
transmit a culture

00:11:33.025 --> 00:11:37.863 align:start size:84% position:16%
is by giving
something beautiful,

00:11:37.863 --> 00:11:41.167 align:start size:94% position:6%
a form of deeper and
richer self understanding

00:11:41.167 --> 00:11:42.635
to an individual, and
that you might say,

00:11:42.635 --> 00:11:44.737
is meant to be what the heart

00:11:44.737 --> 00:11:47.339
or the core activity of
Ralston College is about,

00:11:47.339 --> 00:11:49.175 align:start size:84% position:16%
about giving something
transformative

00:11:49.175 --> 00:11:53.679 align:start size:88% position:13%
in a free and beautiful,
non-instrumental,
non-coercive way

00:11:53.679 --> 00:11:57.083
to help people contend
with the most fundamental

00:11:57.083 --> 00:11:59.485 align:start size:94% position:6%
human questions, and
that takes various forms.

00:11:59.485 --> 00:12:01.687
- Are you surprised
that more schools

00:12:01.687 --> 00:12:06.692 align:start size:97% position:3%
aren't doing this, or abjected
their duty to do this?

00:12:07.793 --> 00:12:11.597 align:start size:84% position:16%
Or is it obvious
because it was the end

00:12:11.597 --> 00:12:14.400
of all of what we now call woke,

00:12:14.400 --> 00:12:16.635
that these ideas got so
into all of the schools

00:12:16.635 --> 00:12:18.704
that it's pretty obvious they
were all gonna end up this way

00:12:18.704 --> 00:12:20.806
and that you'd have to
start a school like this?

00:12:20.806 --> 00:12:24.543 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Well, I think I'd just
say very quickly two things.

00:12:24.543 --> 00:12:28.047
The first is that this is
a multi-factorial problem.

00:12:28.047 --> 00:12:30.282
There's a long history,
and you can talk about

00:12:30.282 --> 00:12:33.652
the vocationalization
of the university,

00:12:33.652 --> 00:12:35.821
that it's partly to do
with trying to educate

00:12:35.821 --> 00:12:39.825 align:start size:88% position:13%
a much wider swath of
our country and culture,

00:12:39.825 --> 00:12:41.827
and that's a noble instinct.

00:12:41.827 --> 00:12:43.395
But it's also kind of
shifted the university

00:12:43.395 --> 00:12:46.232
towards more marketing
and less art history

00:12:46.232 --> 00:12:47.333
or something like that.

00:12:47.333 --> 00:12:49.235
And so that's a question.

00:12:49.235 --> 00:12:51.370 align:start size:94% position:6%
And then you've got the
whole student loan crisis,

00:12:51.370 --> 00:12:54.173 align:start size:97% position:3%
the way the federal government
is radically distorting

00:12:54.173 --> 00:12:57.409 align:start size:94% position:6%
these things and not
only leading to inflation

00:12:57.409 --> 00:12:58.911 align:start size:84% position:16%
and things like
that, but essentially

00:12:58.911 --> 00:13:01.847 align:start size:97% position:3%
there's no normal market
corrective on a poor product

00:13:01.847 --> 00:13:03.916 align:start size:97% position:3%
because the federal government
would just give anyone

00:13:03.916 --> 00:13:06.018
anything they want just
to study at university.

00:13:06.018 --> 00:13:07.253
It's a disaster.

00:13:07.253 --> 00:13:08.687
We actually have way
too many people going

00:13:08.687 --> 00:13:10.489
to get degrees that
are often not worth

00:13:10.489 --> 00:13:12.358
the paper they're printed on,
or not finished or whatever.

00:13:12.358 --> 00:13:13.492
You should look at
the completion rates.

00:13:13.492 --> 00:13:15.361
It's unbelievable nationally.

00:13:15.361 --> 00:13:16.529
And then you've got-

00:13:16.529 --> 00:13:18.030
- How bad are they?

00:13:18.030 --> 00:13:21.934 align:start size:97% position:3%
- I don't want to mis-speak,
but there are many places

00:13:21.934 --> 00:13:24.970 align:start size:84% position:16%
in which dropout rates
are well over 50%,

00:13:24.970 --> 00:13:28.007 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think I'm not mis-speaking
to say well over 50%,

00:13:28.007 --> 00:13:29.441
or incompletion rates.

00:13:29.441 --> 00:13:30.943
And so that's to say
people not finishing

00:13:30.943 --> 00:13:32.411
the degree they started.

00:13:32.411 --> 00:13:34.413
But they still have a loan
for the money that they spent

00:13:34.413 --> 00:13:35.948
on the time that they
don't get degrees.

00:13:35.948 --> 00:13:38.150
- Ironically, with some of
the stuff they're learning,

00:13:38.150 --> 00:13:40.152 align:start position:0%
it's probably not the worst
thing if they're not finishing.

00:13:40.152 --> 00:13:42.388
- Honestly, I think
many, many people

00:13:42.388 --> 00:13:43.856
would be far better off not
going to university at all.

00:13:43.856 --> 00:13:45.424 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think it's an important
point for me to make,

00:13:45.424 --> 00:13:47.793
because here I am part of a
team founding a new college.

00:13:47.793 --> 00:13:49.895
It might make it seem like I
think college is the answer.

00:13:49.895 --> 00:13:52.565
I think by and large,
most, if not most,

00:13:52.565 --> 00:13:56.669 align:start size:84% position:16%
very, very many
people can and should

00:13:56.669 --> 00:13:59.505 align:start position:0%
and will lead higher and
better and more beautiful lives

00:13:59.505 --> 00:14:00.639
by not going to college.

00:14:00.639 --> 00:14:02.174 align:start size:84% position:16%
And we need to
remember that, right?

00:14:02.174 --> 00:14:03.542
We don't want to become a
place that somehow thinks

00:14:03.542 --> 00:14:05.845
we're all just minds, or
that everyone has a mind

00:14:05.845 --> 00:14:07.379 align:start size:84% position:16%
that is good at
thinking about this.

00:14:07.379 --> 00:14:10.482
We have people, in the true
diversity of the human race,

00:14:10.482 --> 00:14:12.484
we need people who are doing
many different kinds of things,

00:14:12.484 --> 00:14:13.552
and most of them, by the way,

00:14:13.552 --> 00:14:15.020
don't involve going to college.

00:14:15.020 --> 00:14:19.191
All that is to say that
in what we're doing,

00:14:21.560 --> 00:14:23.829
we are seeking to be
grounded fundamentally

00:14:23.829 --> 00:14:27.733 align:start size:97% position:3%
in a positive vision, and
relative to this historic arc

00:14:27.733 --> 00:14:29.368
of what's gone wrong
in higher education.

00:14:29.368 --> 00:14:31.203 align:start size:97% position:3%
You can analyze this from
any number of different ways.

00:14:31.203 --> 00:14:32.705
There's the woke
and the ideology

00:14:32.705 --> 00:14:34.006 align:start size:84% position:16%
and the politicization
of education.

00:14:34.006 --> 00:14:35.674
It's a huge problem.

00:14:35.674 --> 00:14:39.879
And whether it is inevitable,
I think actually yes it is.

00:14:39.879 --> 00:14:42.948
I think there is no continuity

00:14:42.948 --> 00:14:45.784 align:start size:81% position:19%
without sort of
birth and rebirth.

00:14:45.784 --> 00:14:48.153 align:start size:97% position:3%
You can look at any
industry, any time, anywhere,

00:14:48.153 --> 00:14:50.422 align:start position:0%
and I think in higher
education in the United States,

00:14:50.422 --> 00:14:52.825 align:start size:84% position:16%
if you want to
really get into it,

00:14:52.825 --> 00:14:54.393
these problems are not new.

00:14:54.393 --> 00:14:56.595 align:start size:84% position:16%
You can go back to,
it's kind of an urtext

00:14:56.595 --> 00:14:58.230
for many small C conservatives,

00:14:58.230 --> 00:15:00.132
is William F. Buckley's
"God and Man at Yale".

00:15:00.132 --> 00:15:03.736
It was written in 1950 or '51
or '52, or very early '50s.

00:15:03.736 --> 00:15:05.437
That's 70 years ago.

00:15:05.437 --> 00:15:08.007
You can look at the most
famous book in this regard,

00:15:08.007 --> 00:15:10.075
is Allan Bloom's "Closing
of the American Mind",

00:15:10.075 --> 00:15:11.810
written in 1985.

00:15:11.810 --> 00:15:13.545
I mean, that's a long time ago.

00:15:13.545 --> 00:15:14.980 align:start size:88% position:13%
You say, well, what have
we done in this time?

00:15:14.980 --> 00:15:17.016 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I think broadly
speaking, people thought,

00:15:17.016 --> 00:15:18.717
well, we can kind of
reform from within.

00:15:18.717 --> 00:15:20.986 align:start position:0%
And I think we should never
give up on a great institution.

00:15:20.986 --> 00:15:23.055
It would be nihilism
to give up on something

00:15:23.055 --> 00:15:25.925
that has been meaningful and
could still be meaningful,

00:15:25.925 --> 00:15:27.593
but at the same time, I
think we have to admit

00:15:27.593 --> 00:15:31.297
that that strategy has
been an abysmal failure,

00:15:31.297 --> 00:15:33.399
because during the time at
which we've been supposedly

00:15:33.399 --> 00:15:35.434 align:start size:88% position:13%
trying to do things
internally, I would say

00:15:35.434 --> 00:15:38.404 align:start size:84% position:16%
the situation at
the universities
has gotten far worse

00:15:38.404 --> 00:15:39.638
in that time than better.

00:15:39.638 --> 00:15:42.107
And so the point
is finally to say

00:15:42.107 --> 00:15:47.112
that I think the current
situation is like the one

00:15:48.280 --> 00:15:49.581 align:start size:81% position:19%
I gave with the
example of hunger.

00:15:49.581 --> 00:15:52.851
Because let me just ask
you whether you would

00:15:52.851 --> 00:15:55.654 align:start size:97% position:3%
agree with this, because you
speak to a lot of people,

00:15:55.654 --> 00:15:57.256 align:start size:81% position:19%
you have a good
sense of the pulse

00:15:57.256 --> 00:16:02.194 align:start position:0%
of a whole generation of people,
and multiple generations.

00:16:03.595 --> 00:16:08.100 align:start size:97% position:3%
I would put it to you that
the average 15 to 25 year old,

00:16:12.571 --> 00:16:14.373 align:start size:88% position:13%
or 30 year old, whatever
you want to say,

00:16:14.373 --> 00:16:16.041 align:start size:84% position:16%
when they're trying
to make sense of life,

00:16:16.041 --> 00:16:20.045
when they're like, oh gosh,
dealing with cancer or death

00:16:20.045 --> 00:16:23.148
or suffering or depression
or my addiction or whatever.

00:16:23.148 --> 00:16:25.217
When I'm trying to
make sense of my life

00:16:25.217 --> 00:16:27.553
in the deepest way, like the
thing that matters the most

00:16:27.553 --> 00:16:29.455
to all of us, is like
can we live a life

00:16:29.455 --> 00:16:31.190 align:start size:84% position:16%
that we regard as
worth living, right?

00:16:31.190 --> 00:16:32.257
There is no more
important question

00:16:32.257 --> 00:16:33.859
for human beings than that.

00:16:33.859 --> 00:16:37.196 align:start size:94% position:6%
I would put it to you that
at the top of the list

00:16:37.196 --> 00:16:40.199
of the places that they would
look is not the university.

00:16:40.199 --> 00:16:42.868
- No, I mean, I agree.

00:16:42.868 --> 00:16:47.139 align:start size:97% position:3%
- And what that means is
that the place that should be

00:16:47.139 --> 00:16:49.808 align:start size:88% position:13%
one of the most obvious
places you'd turn,

00:16:49.808 --> 00:16:51.844
the repository of
all human learning

00:16:51.844 --> 00:16:54.113
and the humanities, which
are nothing but the record

00:16:54.113 --> 00:16:56.248 align:start size:94% position:6%
of what other human
beings have thought about

00:16:56.248 --> 00:16:59.618 align:start size:97% position:3%
their lives in the past, which
is one of the key sources

00:16:59.618 --> 00:17:01.153
to try and understand our lives.

00:17:01.153 --> 00:17:04.089
The point I'm making
is that the industry,

00:17:04.089 --> 00:17:05.624
broadly speaking,
higher education,

00:17:05.624 --> 00:17:08.327
has taken such a wrong turn,
that the thing it should be

00:17:08.327 --> 00:17:11.096 align:start size:94% position:6%
most known for, people
don't even turn to it for.

00:17:11.096 --> 00:17:13.198
It would be like the
last place you turned

00:17:13.198 --> 00:17:14.800
to fill up your car
was a gas station

00:17:14.800 --> 00:17:16.268 align:start size:84% position:16%
because they no
longer had any gas.

00:17:16.268 --> 00:17:18.370 align:start position:0%
- It almost seems like it's
the complete reverse, actually.

00:17:18.370 --> 00:17:19.238
- Yes.

00:17:19.238 --> 00:17:20.439
- It's the 180.

00:17:20.439 --> 00:17:21.373
It's not that it's
just slightly missing

00:17:21.373 --> 00:17:22.608
or they veered off slightly.

00:17:22.608 --> 00:17:24.476
They actually give you
none of those tools.

00:17:24.476 --> 00:17:26.578 align:start size:84% position:16%
They teach you a whole
other set of things

00:17:26.578 --> 00:17:27.946
that have very little to do
with the lessons of the past.

00:17:27.946 --> 00:17:30.582
- In many places, sadly
I think that is true.

00:17:30.582 --> 00:17:33.886 align:start size:97% position:3%
You may end up worse off
intellectually, spiritually,

00:17:33.886 --> 00:17:36.288
morally, by having
your mind corrupted

00:17:36.288 --> 00:17:41.293
by a certain kind of
very downward looking

00:17:42.161 --> 00:17:45.597
cynical nihilism that really,

00:17:45.597 --> 00:17:47.066
and I don't think I can
say this strongly enough,

00:17:47.066 --> 00:17:49.935
because I'm not a political
or instrumental thinker.

00:17:49.935 --> 00:17:52.938 align:start size:84% position:16%
I think we know
that there are ideas

00:17:52.938 --> 00:17:55.207 align:start size:84% position:16%
that are really
toxic for your soul.

00:17:55.207 --> 00:17:58.310
And they lead to bad
places psychologically,

00:17:58.310 --> 00:18:00.412
relationally, intellectually.

00:18:00.412 --> 00:18:03.549
And that's a bad place to be.

00:18:03.549 --> 00:18:06.051
- So when a student
comes to Ralston,

00:18:06.051 --> 00:18:08.454
and you guys have a grad
program right now, right?

00:18:08.454 --> 00:18:10.322
And this is where you
just went to Greece

00:18:10.322 --> 00:18:12.391
with how many kids
did you go with?

00:18:12.391 --> 00:18:14.393 align:start size:97% position:3%
- My first class, we opened
admissions this year,

00:18:14.393 --> 00:18:16.929
our first program is
a one year intensive

00:18:16.929 --> 00:18:18.230
Master's in the Humanities.

00:18:18.230 --> 00:18:20.365
So it's kind of like
an intensive boot camp

00:18:20.365 --> 00:18:23.135 align:start size:84% position:16%
going through the
history of the West.

00:18:23.135 --> 00:18:25.370
It started with two full
months of intensive study

00:18:25.370 --> 00:18:28.140
in Greece of Greek, so
it was kind of boot camp

00:18:28.140 --> 00:18:29.975
in language learning
meets the grand tour.

00:18:29.975 --> 00:18:32.077
It was amazing.

00:18:33.345 --> 00:18:34.780
A full two months.

00:18:34.780 --> 00:18:37.449
We started in Athens,
then a month in Samos,

00:18:37.449 --> 00:18:41.353
the island of Samos, a trip
to Constantinople, Istanbul,

00:18:41.353 --> 00:18:44.089
and then the last three
weeks in partnership

00:18:44.089 --> 00:18:48.894
with the Harvard Center
for Hellenic Studies

00:18:48.894 --> 00:18:50.562
in Nafplio in the Peloponnese.

00:18:50.562 --> 00:18:55.434 align:start size:84% position:16%
So yes, we had a
really intensive on
the ground encounter

00:18:56.635 --> 00:18:58.871
in the place in which
the ideas and ideals,

00:18:58.871 --> 00:19:01.140 align:start size:84% position:16%
you might say
originated in the West

00:19:01.140 --> 00:19:02.608 align:start size:84% position:16%
in the language in
which they originated.

00:19:02.608 --> 00:19:05.244
It's no mistake that
philosophy, theology,

00:19:05.244 --> 00:19:07.713 align:start size:94% position:6%
astronomy, biology, these
are all Greek words.

00:19:07.713 --> 00:19:09.748
So it's not just a
kind of historic,

00:19:09.748 --> 00:19:10.983
oh, that's kind of interesting.

00:19:10.983 --> 00:19:13.118
It's like these are the
categories of thought,

00:19:13.118 --> 00:19:15.187 align:start size:88% position:13%
categories of what does
it mean to be free,

00:19:15.187 --> 00:19:16.488
what does it mean
to be autonomous?

00:19:16.488 --> 00:19:19.024
There's another Greek
word, self ruling.

00:19:19.024 --> 00:19:20.225
That's where it starts.

00:19:20.225 --> 00:19:22.094 align:start size:84% position:16%
So there's a real
sense in which Greece

00:19:22.094 --> 00:19:24.196
and the language of
Greek has always been

00:19:24.196 --> 00:19:26.665 align:start size:84% position:16%
kind of like a
source, like a well

00:19:26.665 --> 00:19:30.869
that perennially enables
us, if we attend to it,

00:19:30.869 --> 00:19:33.405 align:start size:94% position:6%
just as many other things,
history of religion.

00:19:33.405 --> 00:19:35.674 align:start size:94% position:6%
I'm not saying it's all
about Greece all the time,

00:19:35.674 --> 00:19:39.411 align:start size:84% position:16%
but it's a core aspect
of Western culture

00:19:39.411 --> 00:19:42.614
since its early stage
in the 5th century B.C.

00:19:42.614 --> 00:19:44.483 align:start size:94% position:6%
- I did see the movie
"300", and I'm pretty sure

00:19:44.483 --> 00:19:46.885
that's why they were always
taking out Athens, right?

00:19:46.885 --> 00:19:48.587
They were pretty pissed at
these people who had ideas

00:19:48.587 --> 00:19:51.190 align:start size:94% position:6%
and were doing good things
and spreading freedom.

00:19:51.190 --> 00:19:53.192
I mean, I'm half kidding,
actually, but not fully.

00:19:53.192 --> 00:19:57.663 align:start size:97% position:3%
- No, I think if the
battle against the Persians

00:19:57.663 --> 00:20:01.800
had gone differently, the
series of famous battles

00:20:01.800 --> 00:20:03.435
that the Greeks had
no business winning.

00:20:03.435 --> 00:20:05.904
It's like these massive
forces of the empire

00:20:05.904 --> 00:20:10.709 align:start size:97% position:3%
descending on a little group
of this band of citizens

00:20:10.709 --> 00:20:13.812
who just said, give me
freedom or give me death.

00:20:13.812 --> 00:20:16.081 align:start size:81% position:19%
If they had not
won those battles,

00:20:16.081 --> 00:20:18.850
well, there would have been,
I think we can say confidently

00:20:18.850 --> 00:20:20.285
there would have been
no Western culture.

00:20:20.285 --> 00:20:22.554 align:start size:94% position:6%
The ideas of Greece would
not have made it out

00:20:22.554 --> 00:20:25.791 align:start size:88% position:13%
into a wider sphere
that wouldn't have been,

00:20:25.791 --> 00:20:29.294
you might say, the seeds
that laid the groundwork

00:20:29.294 --> 00:20:31.396
for the whole Roman empire.

00:20:31.396 --> 00:20:32.998
They were the seeds
that transformed

00:20:32.998 --> 00:20:34.433
the Christian revelation.

00:20:34.433 --> 00:20:35.867 align:start size:84% position:16%
They were the seeds,
in some respects,

00:20:35.867 --> 00:20:37.669 align:start size:84% position:16%
they were the
ideas through which

00:20:37.669 --> 00:20:39.972 align:start size:84% position:16%
frankly all of the
religions of the book,

00:20:39.972 --> 00:20:41.907
Christianity, Judaism and Islam,

00:20:41.907 --> 00:20:43.709 align:start size:97% position:3%
at certain points of history,
have all been reading

00:20:43.709 --> 00:20:46.678
their books, their holy
books, their Bibles,

00:20:46.678 --> 00:20:48.914
through the lens
of Greek philosophy

00:20:48.914 --> 00:20:50.882
and the categories of thought
we were just talking about.

00:20:50.882 --> 00:20:54.386 align:start position:0%
So the point I'm making is that
you cannot really overstate

00:20:54.386 --> 00:20:57.089
how influential that moment is,

00:20:57.089 --> 00:20:59.691
because in a significant way,

00:20:59.691 --> 00:21:02.394 align:start size:97% position:3%
it is if not one of the
sources from which the whole,

00:21:02.394 --> 00:21:05.230
you might say trunk and
tree of what we now call

00:21:05.230 --> 00:21:06.498
Western culture grows.

00:21:06.498 --> 00:21:07.833 align:start size:84% position:16%
- So you bring these
students there,

00:21:07.833 --> 00:21:09.067
and you were telling
me the other night

00:21:09.067 --> 00:21:10.502
a little bit about
the rigorous process

00:21:10.502 --> 00:21:12.104 align:start size:94% position:6%
to just get in the program
in the first place.

00:21:12.104 --> 00:21:14.006 align:start size:97% position:3%
So they go there, they
learn about the humanities,

00:21:14.006 --> 00:21:18.243
they get the keys to
this great knowledge.

00:21:18.243 --> 00:21:20.045
Are most of them thinking about

00:21:20.045 --> 00:21:21.413
what they're gonna do after?

00:21:21.413 --> 00:21:23.282 align:start size:97% position:3%
Because one of the
questions I get on this show

00:21:23.282 --> 00:21:25.150
all the time when
we do Q and As,

00:21:25.150 --> 00:21:26.785
I get from parents who are like,

00:21:26.785 --> 00:21:28.253 align:start size:88% position:13%
first off, they are
very leery about sending

00:21:28.253 --> 00:21:30.155
their kids to college
in the first place.

00:21:30.155 --> 00:21:32.357
Then the second question
is where do I send them?

00:21:32.357 --> 00:21:33.759 align:start size:84% position:16%
And the third thing
is is anything

00:21:33.759 --> 00:21:35.127
that they're gonna learn
there gonna equip them

00:21:35.127 --> 00:21:36.628
to be out in the real world?

00:21:36.628 --> 00:21:38.363
If you're teaching kids
all about the humanities,

00:21:38.363 --> 00:21:40.732 align:start size:84% position:16%
it's wonderful in
terms of a full life,

00:21:40.732 --> 00:21:42.701
but are they thinking about
what they want to do after,

00:21:42.701 --> 00:21:46.004 align:start size:97% position:3%
or is it almost not that
important yet in some regards?

00:21:46.004 --> 00:21:47.506
- Well, I think.

00:21:47.506 --> 00:21:49.141 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Because you're going
just purely for knowledge,

00:21:49.141 --> 00:21:50.676
it sounds like.

00:21:50.676 --> 00:21:52.144
- Yeah, I think this raises a
really interesting question,

00:21:52.144 --> 00:21:54.680 align:start size:97% position:3%
Dave, because I think we
have to think really honestly

00:21:54.680 --> 00:21:57.416
about what is useful, frankly.

00:21:57.416 --> 00:21:59.785 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I would begin, my
starting position would be

00:22:01.520 --> 00:22:04.690
what matters the most to a
human being has got to be

00:22:04.690 --> 00:22:07.359
at some level what they
regard as most important.

00:22:07.359 --> 00:22:11.163 align:start size:97% position:3%
That's what enables them to
have that perhaps most useful.

00:22:11.163 --> 00:22:14.466
And I really would put it
as a fundamental contention

00:22:14.466 --> 00:22:17.569
that there is nothing that
matters more to human beings,

00:22:17.569 --> 00:22:18.603
we're self conscious creatures,

00:22:18.603 --> 00:22:20.038
we don't just live as automata.

00:22:20.038 --> 00:22:22.407 align:start size:94% position:6%
We think about ourselves,
we value things,

00:22:22.407 --> 00:22:24.009
we feel bad when we do things

00:22:24.009 --> 00:22:25.410 align:start size:84% position:16%
that we think we
shouldn't have done,

00:22:25.410 --> 00:22:27.946
we have hope and
dreams, we love,

00:22:27.946 --> 00:22:32.084
we love beauty, we
gather in community.

00:22:32.084 --> 00:22:33.919
What I would say is there
is nothing more important

00:22:33.919 --> 00:22:35.954 align:start size:84% position:16%
than giving a human
being the tools

00:22:35.954 --> 00:22:38.423
for them to regard their
own life as meaningful.

00:22:38.423 --> 00:22:42.260
And that doesn't come down
to simply material things.

00:22:42.260 --> 00:22:43.762
I mean, you can have
all the material wealth

00:22:43.762 --> 00:22:47.499
and success in the world and
feel like you are poor inside.

00:22:52.204 --> 00:22:55.674
You can not have
the relationships.

00:22:55.674 --> 00:22:57.175 align:start size:84% position:16%
No one gets to their
death bed saying,

00:22:57.175 --> 00:22:58.744
oh gosh, I wish I just
made a little more money.

00:22:58.744 --> 00:23:00.245 align:start size:88% position:13%
I mean, they get to
their death bed saying,

00:23:00.245 --> 00:23:02.381 align:start size:84% position:16%
they're still thinking
about things like

00:23:02.381 --> 00:23:04.449 align:start size:94% position:6%
why did I never reconcile
with my brother,

00:23:04.449 --> 00:23:06.151
and how could I have done so?

00:23:06.151 --> 00:23:08.220
And so I really want
to explode this idea

00:23:08.220 --> 00:23:10.889 align:start size:97% position:3%
that the humanities are
somehow, and I'm not saying

00:23:10.889 --> 00:23:12.691
you're suggesting this,
but as if they're somehow

00:23:12.691 --> 00:23:15.427 align:start size:84% position:16%
this recherche
thing, oh you know,

00:23:15.427 --> 00:23:17.763 align:start size:94% position:6%
we've imagined an English
accent or something.

00:23:17.763 --> 00:23:19.731
We're at a cocktail party
talking about something

00:23:19.731 --> 00:23:21.199
that no one gives a damn about.

00:23:21.199 --> 00:23:23.268
No, I'm talking about the
fundamental questions,

00:23:23.268 --> 00:23:25.971
truth, justice, love,
beauty, forgiveness.

00:23:25.971 --> 00:23:26.905
How do these things work?

00:23:26.905 --> 00:23:27.739
What are they?

00:23:27.739 --> 00:23:29.207
And that's the bedrock.

00:23:29.207 --> 00:23:30.375
And so I'd say that, but
then I'd also say something

00:23:30.375 --> 00:23:31.676 align:start size:84% position:16%
more practical
towards your question,

00:23:31.676 --> 00:23:36.681
which is it's easy to
think that the best way

00:23:37.749 --> 00:23:39.818 align:start size:88% position:13%
to do something
practical is practical.

00:23:39.818 --> 00:23:43.054
And in fact, this is the
really interesting thing

00:23:43.054 --> 00:23:44.289
about the humanities.

00:23:44.289 --> 00:23:45.490
If you subordinate them

00:23:45.490 --> 00:23:47.325 align:start size:84% position:16%
to a practical
instrumental outcome,

00:23:47.325 --> 00:23:48.560 align:start size:84% position:16%
like you say I'm
gonna read Shakespeare

00:23:48.560 --> 00:23:50.061
because it'll help me
to teach marketing,

00:23:50.061 --> 00:23:52.230
it'll help me to learn how
to sell razors or something,

00:23:52.230 --> 00:23:55.967
it's gonna fall through
your hands like sand.

00:23:55.967 --> 00:23:57.269
You're not gonna
have anything left,

00:23:57.269 --> 00:23:58.804
because you're not
gonna get anything

00:23:58.804 --> 00:24:00.305
out of damn Shakespeare
if you read him that way.

00:24:00.305 --> 00:24:03.308
Whereas if you really attend
to this amazing understanding

00:24:04.776 --> 00:24:07.112
of the human condition,
you really get into that,

00:24:07.112 --> 00:24:09.881
well you know, that may
make you quite powerful

00:24:09.881 --> 00:24:12.317 align:start size:97% position:3%
when you start thinking
about what motivates people,

00:24:12.317 --> 00:24:13.919
how do we speak to people.

00:24:13.919 --> 00:24:15.987
I'm not saying this is gonna
help you sell a million razors,

00:24:15.987 --> 00:24:17.389
but it very well may.

00:24:17.389 --> 00:24:18.590 align:start size:97% position:3%
And so the point I'm
making is that if you don't

00:24:18.590 --> 00:24:19.791
subordinate it practically,

00:24:19.791 --> 00:24:21.893 align:start size:84% position:16%
it becomes
unbelievably powerful.

00:24:21.893 --> 00:24:24.129
I mean, let's just be
honest about the fact

00:24:24.129 --> 00:24:26.832 align:start size:94% position:6%
that the kind of education
I'm talking about

00:24:26.832 --> 00:24:30.335
is in fact the education
that has basically defined

00:24:31.770 --> 00:24:36.308
statesmen, politicians, the
movers and shakers and doers,

00:24:39.077 --> 00:24:42.113
in many respects, of
our whole history.

00:24:42.113 --> 00:24:45.617
So it's actually a
very recent idea,

00:24:45.617 --> 00:24:50.622 align:start size:97% position:3%
and I think it's profoundly
mistaken to think

00:24:52.057 --> 00:24:53.492
that the way in which you
can do something important,

00:24:53.492 --> 00:24:56.995
even in the real world,
is you've got to start

00:24:56.995 --> 00:24:58.697
by understanding that
world fundamentally.

00:24:58.697 --> 00:25:00.932
- Right, well that's why
it's so interesting to me,

00:25:00.932 --> 00:25:03.935
well, I guess it's interesting
but obvious at the same time

00:25:03.935 --> 00:25:05.537 align:start size:88% position:13%
that Jordan is
involved with you guys,

00:25:05.537 --> 00:25:07.839
because the tools that
he's always talking about

00:25:07.839 --> 00:25:11.009
for someone to take
their life seriously,

00:25:11.009 --> 00:25:13.445 align:start size:84% position:16%
well, if you study the
humanities honestly

00:25:13.445 --> 00:25:15.514
and really get an assessment
of history and everything else,

00:25:15.514 --> 00:25:17.816
and then you have a
little of the history

00:25:17.816 --> 00:25:19.618
and the knowledge before you,

00:25:19.618 --> 00:25:21.620
and then you start doing
those things yourself,

00:25:21.620 --> 00:25:22.954
then I think to your point,

00:25:22.954 --> 00:25:26.391
in some ways that is the
tool set that you need,

00:25:26.391 --> 00:25:28.760
regardless of what you
studied specifically,

00:25:28.760 --> 00:25:30.295
as per a vocation.

00:25:30.295 --> 00:25:31.496 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah, I think that's
completely right,

00:25:31.496 --> 00:25:32.964 align:start size:84% position:16%
and I think when
you look at, I mean,

00:25:32.964 --> 00:25:35.267 align:start size:97% position:3%
our students, they came
from tech and from teaching,

00:25:35.267 --> 00:25:39.404
from the art world,
from a range of both,

00:25:39.404 --> 00:25:42.007
you might say, pretty
hardcore practical

00:25:42.007 --> 00:25:45.310
to education and so on,
and I think their careers

00:25:45.310 --> 00:25:47.412
are likely to be
as diverse as that.

00:25:47.412 --> 00:25:49.381 align:start size:84% position:16%
But just even to
speak as an employer,

00:25:49.381 --> 00:25:51.816
and I know that as you are,

00:25:51.816 --> 00:25:54.886 align:start size:84% position:16%
you can't just
take it for granted

00:25:54.886 --> 00:25:56.888
that someone can
write a sentence

00:25:56.888 --> 00:25:58.590 align:start size:81% position:19%
in our current day
and age, right?

00:25:58.590 --> 00:26:02.894 align:start size:88% position:13%
But speaking as someone
who hires people,

00:26:02.894 --> 00:26:05.030 align:start size:94% position:6%
it's actually very
important that you be able

00:26:05.030 --> 00:26:08.066
to convey not only
something grammatical

00:26:08.066 --> 00:26:11.336
and not riddled with mistakes,
but maybe of a certain tone.

00:26:11.336 --> 00:26:14.806
Imagine how valuable that is,

00:26:14.806 --> 00:26:18.777 align:start size:97% position:3%
if in your life you're
able to be a kind of master

00:26:18.777 --> 00:26:21.880
of how you present what you say

00:26:21.880 --> 00:26:24.149 align:start size:84% position:16%
so it can be maximally
well taken on

00:26:24.149 --> 00:26:25.584 align:start size:84% position:16%
by the person
you're saying it to.

00:26:25.584 --> 00:26:26.985 align:start size:97% position:3%
That's about the most
useful thing you could have,

00:26:26.985 --> 00:26:28.853 align:start size:88% position:13%
particularly in a so
called knowledge economy

00:26:28.853 --> 00:26:30.355
we're living in now.

00:26:30.355 --> 00:26:33.558 align:start size:97% position:3%
So I don't want to undersell
the really practical side

00:26:35.594 --> 00:26:37.395 align:start size:84% position:16%
of a rigorous
humanities education.

00:26:37.395 --> 00:26:38.830
But you don't get that
if you don't approach it

00:26:38.830 --> 00:26:40.765
with serious rigor
in the first place.

00:26:40.765 --> 00:26:43.268
- Just a couple more we
can do somewhat briefly.

00:26:43.268 --> 00:26:46.738 align:start position:0%
I assume you're getting
probably calls every single day

00:26:46.738 --> 00:26:51.009
from professors who are like,
I cannot take this anymore.

00:26:51.009 --> 00:26:52.344
Hire me, please.

00:26:52.344 --> 00:26:55.080
I mean, is that part of
what your job now is?

00:26:55.080 --> 00:26:57.015
- Certainly, and we're
really looking forward

00:26:57.015 --> 00:27:01.052
to continuing to add to
our team as we expand.

00:27:01.052 --> 00:27:03.254 align:start size:94% position:6%
There is a lot of
dissatisfaction out there.

00:27:05.056 --> 00:27:07.292 align:start size:97% position:3%
We've had, of course,
hundreds and hundreds of CVs

00:27:07.292 --> 00:27:10.662
over the months and
the last few years.

00:27:10.662 --> 00:27:12.530
That, I think, is fundamentally,

00:27:12.530 --> 00:27:14.065 align:start size:88% position:13%
it's really interesting
to ask why that is,

00:27:14.065 --> 00:27:17.268 align:start size:88% position:13%
and I think it's because
there's these people

00:27:17.268 --> 00:27:18.837 align:start size:84% position:16%
that they actually
dedicated their lives,

00:27:18.837 --> 00:27:23.675 align:start size:88% position:13%
because they said I
want to be part of this

00:27:23.675 --> 00:27:26.311 align:start size:97% position:3%
sort of beautiful transmission
and opening up the minds

00:27:26.311 --> 00:27:28.546
of the young to things
that will help them.

00:27:28.546 --> 00:27:30.448 align:start size:88% position:13%
And often they find
themselves in positions

00:27:30.448 --> 00:27:33.018
in which they don't feel
they can speak their mind,

00:27:33.018 --> 00:27:35.987
they're ostracized
by their colleagues

00:27:35.987 --> 00:27:40.392
or fearful of speaking openly,
where they're under pressure

00:27:40.392 --> 00:27:42.894 align:start size:84% position:16%
to politicize their
own classrooms.

00:27:42.894 --> 00:27:46.197 align:start size:88% position:13%
Its just a sort of
sorry position to be in,

00:27:46.197 --> 00:27:50.802
where you're not able to
really devote yourself

00:27:50.802 --> 00:27:54.773
to the high order things that
inspired your own vocation.

00:27:54.773 --> 00:27:58.109
And we hope that, not
only do we look forward

00:27:58.109 --> 00:27:59.310
to hiring many of those people,

00:27:59.310 --> 00:28:02.547
but we also hope that
in some even small

00:28:02.547 --> 00:28:06.418 align:start size:94% position:6%
and maybe not so small
way that what we're doing,

00:28:06.418 --> 00:28:08.053 align:start size:88% position:13%
what other new
institutions are doing,

00:28:08.053 --> 00:28:12.223
may play some role
in recollecting these
other institutions

00:28:12.223 --> 00:28:14.492
back to what they're all about.

00:28:14.492 --> 00:28:16.628 align:start size:94% position:6%
- How hard is it building
new institutions?

00:28:16.628 --> 00:28:18.129
I mean, I know a
little something

00:28:18.129 --> 00:28:19.831 align:start size:88% position:13%
about building new
things on the tech side.

00:28:19.831 --> 00:28:21.633
It's tough, and you
will get pushback

00:28:21.633 --> 00:28:23.868 align:start size:84% position:16%
in every possible
way you can think of,

00:28:23.868 --> 00:28:25.603
and a whole bunch that
you cannot think of.

00:28:25.603 --> 00:28:26.971
But you still do it.

00:28:26.971 --> 00:28:29.040 align:start size:84% position:16%
- I think it's very
hard, is what I think.

00:28:29.040 --> 00:28:32.077 align:start size:94% position:6%
I'm not gonna sit here and
pretend it's been easy.

00:28:32.077 --> 00:28:33.945
Of course, we've
been very fortunate

00:28:33.945 --> 00:28:38.950
to attract a network of
support and friendship

00:28:40.418 --> 00:28:43.221
and people who are behind us,
but that took years to build.

00:28:43.221 --> 00:28:47.492
And I think it's
worth saying that

00:28:48.827 --> 00:28:50.795
that, I think, is also a symptom

00:28:50.795 --> 00:28:55.233
of something that's gone very
badly awry in our country.

00:28:55.233 --> 00:28:59.504
In the 19th century, there
were something like a dozen,

00:28:59.504 --> 00:29:02.607 align:start size:97% position:3%
25, if you look at the
numbers, they were starting

00:29:02.607 --> 00:29:04.876 align:start size:97% position:3%
new colleges and
universities at a real clip.

00:29:04.876 --> 00:29:06.111 align:start size:84% position:16%
Every few months,
there was another one.

00:29:06.111 --> 00:29:07.545
They're the ones we
have now, by the way.

00:29:07.545 --> 00:29:09.848 align:start size:94% position:6%
Most of them were started
in the 19th century.

00:29:09.848 --> 00:29:14.419 align:start size:97% position:3%
And you've got to ask
yourself what has gone wrong

00:29:14.419 --> 00:29:17.055
when on some of these
really core issues

00:29:17.055 --> 00:29:19.891 align:start size:97% position:3%
of what we are as a culture,
we've lost the dynamism

00:29:19.891 --> 00:29:21.392
to recreate and reinvent.

00:29:21.392 --> 00:29:24.295 align:start size:97% position:3%
There's a kind of surrender,
a kind of capitulation

00:29:24.295 --> 00:29:26.364
to well, what can we do?

00:29:26.364 --> 00:29:27.966 align:start size:97% position:3%
And it's kind of like
you're on the Titanic, man,

00:29:27.966 --> 00:29:30.235
and you're saying, well, I
guess let's just go down.

00:29:30.235 --> 00:29:32.370
I mean, do you see
what I'm saying?

00:29:34.072 --> 00:29:38.877
I think we need to
be able to recover.

00:29:38.877 --> 00:29:41.579 align:start size:84% position:16%
It's sort of like
remembering yourself.

00:29:41.579 --> 00:29:44.816
You're sort of in lethargy
or depression or whatever,

00:29:44.816 --> 00:29:46.885 align:start size:84% position:16%
and you remember,
you come alive again.

00:29:46.885 --> 00:29:48.753
I think that's what
we need as a culture.

00:29:48.753 --> 00:29:52.690
And I don't think you can do
that if you have pessimism

00:29:52.690 --> 00:29:54.993 align:start size:84% position:16%
